<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
	The app runs sandboxed, in its own data container, with no privilege
	beyond reaching the daemon and driving the GPU.

	This is the architecture stated plainly in a signature: the app renders
	and nothing else, and every privileged operation — spawning a shell,
	owning a PTY, running as another user — happens in ighosttyd, which is
	already root and already authenticates its peers. An app that cannot
	spawn does not need the entitlements that make spawning possible, and an
	app that keeps its container keeps its preferences somewhere only it can
	write.

	So the list is: the marker the daemon authenticates against, permission
	to look up the daemon's mach service, the identity the sandbox matches
	against this app's container, and the GPU user clients Metal opens.

	The last two are what an ldid-signed install has to say out loud, both
	proven on device (iPad8,9, iOS 18.5, rootless bootstrap, 2026-08-18):

	- `application-identifier` — without it the process has no application
	  identity, so the sandbox cannot match it to the container installd
	  registered for this bundle id, and the kernel denies the app writes to
	  its OWN container: `deny(1) file-write-create
	  .../Containers/Data/Application/<uuid>/Library/…`, tmp, Caches, all of
	  it. libghostty must write its config to a file to load it, so a
	  terminal never boots: `ghostty_app_new` is never reached, every surface
	  rebuild fails, and the interface sits on "Starting…" forever. A signed
	  App Store build gets this entitlement from its provisioning profile as
	  `TEAMID.bundle.id`; an ad-hoc signature has to carry the bare bundle id
	  instead. This is the entitlement that keeps the app sandboxed — the
	  alternative fix, `no-sandbox` plus `storage.AppDataContainers`, buys
	  the same writes by giving up the boundary this project is built on.
	- `com.apple.security.iokit-user-client-class` — the bootstrap withholds
	  the GPU from a non-platform binary. The kernel says what to do about
	  it in as many words: `deny(1) iokit-open-user-client
	  AGXDeviceUserClient … This must be in your
	  com.apple.security.iokit-user-client-class entitlement`. Without it
	  Metal cannot create a device (`Failed to create an IOAccelDevice…
	  IOServiceOpen returned kIOReturn(0xE00002E2)`), ghostty logs
	  `error.MetalFailed`, and the terminal is a black square that never
	  reports a viewport. The list is the render path only — Apple's GPU
	  (AGX), its older IOAccel vocabulary, IOSurface, the framebuffer, JPEG
	  decode, and the HID clients UIKit opens for input.

	Entitlements deliberately NOT here, and what would bring each one back:

	- `platform-application` — grants nothing the app needs and actively
	  tightens its sandbox. Add it back only if the bootstrap refuses to
	  launch a non-platform binary.
	- `com.apple.private.security.no-sandbox` and its container-removing
	  siblings `no-container` / `container-required=false` /
	  `storage.AppDataContainers` — the sandbox is the point. Everything
	  they would fix, `application-identifier` fixes without giving it up.
	- `com.apple.private.skip-library-validation` — add back only if the app
	  fails to launch because a framework it loads is refused.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>application-identifier</key>
	<string>wiki.qaq.iGhostty</string>
	<key>wiki.qaq.ighostty.client</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.exception.mach-lookup.global-name</key>
	<array>
		<string>wiki.qaq.ighostty.service</string>
	</array>
	<key>com.apple.security.iokit-user-client-class</key>
	<array>
		<string>AGXCommandQueue</string>
		<string>AGXDevice</string>
		<string>AGXDeviceUserClient</string>
		<string>AGXSharedUserClient</string>
		<string>AGXGLContext</string>
		<string>IOAccelContext</string>
		<string>IOAccelContext2</string>
		<string>IOAccelDevice</string>
		<string>IOAccelDevice2</string>
		<string>IOAccelSharedUserClient</string>
		<string>IOAccelSharedUserClient2</string>
		<string>IOAccelSubmitter2</string>
		<string>IOSurfaceRootUserClient</string>
		<string>IOSurfaceAcceleratorClient</string>
		<string>IOMobileFramebufferUserClient</string>
		<string>AppleJPEGDriverUserClient</string>
		<string>IOHIDLibUserClient</string>
		<string>IOHIDEventServiceFastPathUserClient</string>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
